"""Phase 2.1 conversation batch H: extra distinct styles - terse, skeptical, multi-part questions, role-play-free creative help, mid-chat reversals."""
from gen21_common import *

B = Bank("general_conversation", "c21h")
d = B.d

d("multi_part", "Three quick things: what's a good first pet, how often do I walk a dog, and is a hamster nocturnal?", "1) Good first pets: a cat or a small dog if you have time, or a fish or rats if you want something lower maintenance. 2) Most dogs need at least two walks a day, 20 to 60 minutes each depending on breed and age. 3) Yes, hamsters are mostly nocturnal, so they'll be most active in the evening and at night.")
d("multi_part", "Can you tell me (a) why bread rises and (b) why my loaf came out flat?", "(a) Yeast eats sugars in the dough and releases carbon dioxide, which gets trapped by the gluten network and inflates the bread. (b) The usual suspects for a flat loaf are old or dead yeast, water that was too hot (it kills yeast), too little kneading, over-proofing so the structure collapses, or too much flour. Tell me what the crumb looked like and I can narrow it down.")
d("skeptical", "Honestly, do vitamins even work?", "It depends. If you have a deficiency (vitamin D in winter, B12 on a vegan diet, iron after heavy periods), supplements help. For people with a balanced diet, large trials have mostly found little benefit from a daily multivitamin. A blood test and your doctor can tell you if you actually need something.")
d("skeptical", "I don't trust horoscopes. Can you explain why they feel accurate?", "Part of it is the Barnum effect: descriptions are vague enough that most people see themselves in them (\"you sometimes doubt yourself, but you're capable of more\"). Add confirmation bias, where you remember the hits and forget the misses, and the fact that you read your own sign looking for a match. Controlled tests haven't found that signs predict personality or events.")
d("skeptical", "is a detox tea actually a thing", "Not really. Your liver and kidneys already do the detoxing. Most detox teas contain laxatives or diuretics, so the weight you lose is mostly water, and they can cause cramps and dehydration. If you want to feel better, focus on water, fibre, sleep and activity.")
d("skeptical", "People say you should drink 8 glasses of water. Where does that come from?", "There's no solid scientific origin. It probably traces to a 1945 US recommendation of about 2.5 litres a day, which noted that most of it comes from food, and the last part was forgotten. Needs vary, and thirst plus pale urine is a reasonable guide.")
d("creative_help", "I need a slogan for a lemonade stand run by my kids", "Some options: \"Sour days, sweet help\", \"When life gives us lemons, we give you lemonade\", \"Squeezed fresh, served with a smile\", or \"Cold, tart, and kid-approved\". Would you like something funnier or more heartwarming?")
d("creative_help", "Give me three first lines for a mystery story.", "1. \"The last person to see Eleanor alive was the clock in the hall, and it had stopped at 9:14.\"\n2. \"Everyone in the village agreed the stranger had arrived on Tuesday; nobody could agree on which Tuesday.\"\n3. \"The letter was addressed to a man who had been dead for eleven years, and it was in my own handwriting.\"")
d("creative_help", "write a limerick about a forgetful cat", "A cat who forgot where she'd been\nWalked in circles, then purred, quite serene.\nShe'd forgotten her bowl,\nBut remembered her goal:\nTo be fed, and then not to be seen.")
d("creative_help", "Help me think of a theme for my daughter's 10th birthday. She likes science and art.", "How about a \"Mad Scientist Art Lab\": colourful slime and baking-soda volcanoes, a marble-painting or spin-art station, and rainbow density jars made from sugar water and food colouring. Guests could wear lab coats (old white shirts) and take home a decorated mini notebook as the party favour.")
d("creative_help", "I want a motto for my study group, something short", "\"Learn together, pass together.\" Or for a lighter tone: \"Fuelled by coffee, powered by flashcards.\"")
d("creative_help", "Can you write a short toast for my brother's wedding? He's marrying Dani. They met climbing.", "Friends and family, please raise your glasses. Mark and Dani met on a rock wall, and I think that says everything: they trust each other with their lives, they cheer each other on at every hard move, and they never stop looking for the next summit together. Dani, welcome to the family. Mark, you finally found someone who keeps up. To the happy couple!")
d("mid_chat_reversal", "What's a good name for a bakery?", "Something like \"Golden Crumb\" or \"The Rising Loaf\". Is it for a real shop?", "scratch that, it's a fictional bakery in my novel", "Then you can be more playful or telling: \"Mrs Pemberton's Second-Chance Buns\" suggests a character and a past; \"The Flour & Feather\" feels whimsical; \"Crumbs\" is plain and cosy. Tell me the setting and tone of the novel and I'll match it.")
d("mid_chat_reversal", "How do I get from the airport to the city centre in Berlin?", "From BER, the Airport Express train (FEX) reaches the main station in around 30 minutes, and S-Bahn lines also run to the centre. Buy a ticket for zones A, B and C before you board.", "oh sorry, I meant Hamburg", "In Hamburg, the S-Bahn line S1 goes from the airport to the central station in about 25 minutes. Tickets are sold at the machines, and the day ticket is usually worth it if you plan several rides. Check the HVV site for current fares and times.")
d("mid_chat_reversal", "I think I'll quit my job tomorrow!", "That's a big step. Do you have another job lined up or savings to cover a few months? And has something specific happened today that is driving this?", "my boss yelled at me in front of everyone", "That's humiliating, and being angry is understandable. A decision made in the heat of that moment is often one you rethink after a night's sleep. Consider writing down what happened, then deciding tomorrow whether this is a pattern, whether HR is an option, and whether you're ready financially to leave. Quitting may still be right, but let it be a choice, not a reaction.")
d("compare", "iPhone or Android?", "Both are excellent. iPhone: consistent updates for many years, tight integration with other Apple devices, simple to use. Android: more variety in price and hardware, more customisation, and wider options for file handling. If you already own a Mac or iPad, an iPhone fits well. Otherwise, choose based on budget and which apps or features matter to you.")
d("compare", "Is it better to take the stairs or the elevator?", "For your health, stairs: they are brief exercise that adds up, and they don't cost extra time on a few floors. Take the elevator when you're carrying heavy loads, have a mobility issue, or it's many floors, and use stairs for the last few.")
d("compare", "paper books vs e-readers?", "Paper is pleasant, needs no battery, and tends to give a better sense of place in the book. E-readers are lighter, hold thousands of books, let you adjust the font size and read in the dark. Many people use both: an e-reader for travel and commuting, paper for favourites.")
d("compare", "Should I buy a standing desk?", "It can help if you sit all day, mainly by encouraging you to change position. The benefit comes from moving: alternating between standing and sitting every 30 to 60 minutes, and taking walks, not from standing all day. If budget is tight, a stack of boxes on a table is an inexpensive test before buying.")
d("compare", "Tea or coffee in the morning?", "Coffee has more caffeine, so it wakes you up faster; tea has less and contains L-theanine, which many people find gives calmer focus. If coffee makes you jittery or affects your sleep, try tea. There is no wrong answer.")
d("fact_check", "Is it true that we lose most of our body heat through our heads?", "No. The head loses about as much heat as any other uncovered body part in proportion to its area, roughly 7 to 10%. The idea stuck from a military experiment where subjects wore warm suits but no hats. In the cold, cover everything.")
d("fact_check", "Does shaving make hair grow back thicker?", "No. Shaving cuts hair at the surface, leaving a blunt tip that can feel coarser as it grows out, but it doesn't change the thickness, colour or growth rate of the hair.")
d("fact_check", "Do we really use only a small part of our brains?", "No. Imaging shows that nearly all regions of the brain are active over a day, and even during sleep. Damage to almost any area has an effect. The 10% idea is a myth.")
d("fact_check", "Is the Great Barrier Reef one coral?", "No. It is a huge system of nearly 3,000 individual reefs and about 900 islands, stretching over 2,300 km off Queensland, Australia, and built by billions of tiny coral polyps.")
d("fact_check", "Does reading in dim light ruin your eyes?", "Reading in dim light can make your eyes tired and dry, but there's no good evidence that it causes permanent damage. Good lighting makes it more comfortable.")
d("fact_check", "Do you need to wait 24 hours before reporting a missing person?", "No. That's a TV myth. In most places you can and should report a missing person immediately, especially a child or vulnerable adult. Contact the police right away.")
d("advice_specific", "My toddler's first day at daycare is tomorrow and I'm more nervous than he is", "That's very common, and it helps him that you're calm. Keep goodbyes short and warm (a quick hug and a clear \"I'll be back after lunch\"), avoid sneaking away, and bring a familiar comfort item. Many daycares let you phone to check later. Crying at drop-off often stops within minutes.")
d("advice_specific", "I'm hosting Thanksgiving for the first time, 12 people. Where do I start?", "Work backwards from the meal time. Count the days: shop and order the turkey early (about 1 lb per person), thaw in the fridge (about 24 hours per 4 to 5 lb), and plan side dishes that can be made ahead (cranberry sauce, pies, stuffing prep). Make a timeline for the day, ask guests to bring a dish, and borrow chairs or dishes now. Which part worries you most?")
d("advice_specific", "I have to give a eulogy and I don't know where to start", "I'm sorry for your loss. Keep it simple: begin with who they were to you, share two or three short stories that show their character, and end with what you'll carry forward. Aim for 3 to 5 minutes, which is about 400 to 600 words. Write it as you would speak to the person's family, and read it aloud once beforehand. If you tell me about them, I can help you shape it.")
d("advice_specific", "My bike's chain keeps falling off", "Common causes are a worn chain or cassette, a bent derailleur hanger, a misadjusted front or rear derailleur limit screw, or a chain that's too long. Start by cleaning and lubricating the chain, checking it for stiff links, and adjusting the limit screws so the chain can't overshoot the gear. If it persists, a bike shop can check chain wear with a gauge.")
d("advice_specific", "How do I deal with a really long commute?", "Make the time useful or restful: audiobooks, podcasts or language practice if you drive; reading or work if you take transit. Try shifting your hours to avoid the peak, negotiating a remote day, or finding a route with fewer stops. If it's wearing you down, count the real cost in time and energy when comparing jobs or homes.")
d("advice_specific", "How can I help a friend who's grieving?", "Show up and listen more than you advise. Offer specific help instead of \"let me know\": \"I'm dropping off dinner Thursday\" or \"I can walk the dog this week\". Say their person's name, don't rush them to feel better, and keep checking in weeks later, when everyone else has gone back to normal.")
d("explain_step", "Walk me through how to calculate a percentage change", "Subtract the old value from the new value, divide by the old value, then multiply by 100. Example: a price goes from 80 to 100. The change is 100 - 80 = 20; 20 / 80 = 0.25; times 100 gives a 25% increase. If it fell from 100 to 80, it's (80 - 100) / 100 = -20%. Notice that going up 25% and then down 25% does not return you to the start.")
d("explain_step", "How does a thermostat work?", "A thermostat measures room temperature and compares it with your setting. When the room is colder than the setpoint, it switches the heating on; when it is warm enough, it switches it off. Most allow a small margin (hysteresis) so the heating doesn't flick on and off constantly.")
d("explain_step", "how does GPS know where I am", "Satellites in orbit broadcast their position and the exact time. Your phone receives signals from at least four satellites, measures how long each took to arrive, and converts those delays into distances. Combining the distances (trilateration) gives your position. GPS doesn't send anything from your phone to the satellites.")
d("explain_step", "Explain how a credit card transaction works", "When you pay, the merchant's terminal sends the details to its payment processor, which asks your card network (such as Visa or Mastercard) to check with your bank. Your bank verifies the account and funds or credit limit and approves or declines in seconds. Later, in a batch, the money moves between banks, and the merchant is paid minus fees.")
d("explain_step", "How do noise-cancelling headphones work?", "Microphones pick up outside sound, and electronics generate a sound wave that is the inverse (opposite phase) of it. When the two meet, they largely cancel each other. This works best on steady low-frequency sounds like engine hum, and less well on sudden or high-pitched noise.")
d("explain_step", "How do I calculate my age in days?", "Multiply your age in years by 365, then add one day for each leap year you've lived through (roughly age / 4). A quick estimate for 30 years: 30 x 365 = 10,950, plus about 7 leap days, giving about 10,957 days. For an exact answer, use a date calculator with your birth date.")
d("support_tone", "I messed up an important email and sent it to the wrong person", "That's stressful, but it happens to many people. If your email system supports recall and it's quick, try it, though it often fails. Otherwise, send a short note to the recipient asking them to delete it and not share it, and tell your manager or IT team if it contains sensitive information. Then add a safeguard such as a delay on sending.")
d("support_tone", "I think I offended my friend and I don't know how to fix it", "A short, sincere message usually helps: \"I've been thinking about what I said, and I think it hurt you. I'm sorry. I'd like to hear how it landed for you.\" Avoid explaining your intentions first or adding \"but\". Then listen, and give them time.")
d("support_tone", "everything feels pointless lately", "I'm sorry you're feeling that way. That flat, empty feeling is worth taking seriously, especially if it's lasted a while. Is there anyone you can talk to? If it's persisting or you ever feel unsafe or think about harming yourself, please reach out to a doctor or a local crisis line right away. I'm here to talk, too. What's been going on?")

if __name__ == "__main__":
    save("conv21_h", B.records())
